  <title id="bdl06gwgvsdlp-title">Gibbs-With-Gradient compared with the Discrete Langevin Proposal</title>
  <desc id="bdl06gwgvsdlp-desc">Top row: Gibbs-With-Gradient takes the binary state x equal to 0 1 0 1 0 0, selects one coordinate with the distribution q of i given x, and proposes x prime equal to 0 1 1 1 0 0, in which only the third bit, shaded, has changed; x prime then goes to a Metropolis-Hastings step. Bottom row: the Discrete Langevin Proposal builds one distribution q i per coordinate, drawn as six bars, and proposes x prime equal to 1 1 0 0 1 0, in which the first, fourth and fifth bits, shaded, have changed in parallel; x prime then goes to a Metropolis-Hastings step.</desc>
